   "source": [
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
    "X = cafe[['temperature_c']]; y = cafe['pastry_demand']\n",
    "lin = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)\n",
    "print(f'pastry_demand ≈ {lin.intercept_:.1f} + {lin.coef_[0]:.2f} × temperature')\n",
    "print('predicted demand at 20°C:', round(lin.predict(pd.DataFrame({'temperature_c': [20]}))[0]))\n",
    "sns.regplot(data=cafe, x='temperature_c', y='pastry_demand',\n",
    "            scatter_kws={'alpha':.3}, line_kws={'color':'crimson'})\n",
    "plt.title('Regression turns a correlation into a prediction'); plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "127f256c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Reading the result**\n",
    "- The fitted line is roughly $\\widehat{\\text{pastry demand}} \\approx 77.9 + 0.58 \\times \\text{temperature}$.\n",
    "- Slope ≈ 0.58: each extra °C is linked to about 0.6 more pastries per day.\n",
    "- Prediction at 20 °C: $77.9 + 0.58 \\times 20 \\approx 89.5$, i.e. about 89 pastries.\n",
    "- Here $r \\approx 0.32$, so $R^2 \\approx 0.10$: temperature explains only ~10 % of the variation. The line is useful\n",
    "  but far from complete — other factors (e.g. foot traffic, weekends) matter more.\n",
    "\n",
    "> Regression predicts; it does not prove causation (Notebook 3 still applies)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f0281b30",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 2 — Correlation and Machine Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "In machine learning, **features** are the input variables and the **target** is what we predict.\n",
    "Correlation helps with **feature selection** in three ways:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Find strong predictors** — features with a large $|r|$ with the target\n",
    "2. **Spot redundant (collinear) features** — features strongly correlated with *each other* carry the same\n",
    "   information; keeping both adds little and can make models harder to interpret\n",
    "3. **Drop useless features** — $r \\approx 0$ with the target (but remember $r$ only detects *linear* links)\n",
    "\n",
    "Our target is `daily_revenue`. Let's rank the candidate features by their correlation with it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "cb6762d6",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-22T08:16:04.070948Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-22T08:16:04.070369Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-22T08:16:04.079672Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-22T08:16:04.078420Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
