      "r(temperature, iced_drink_sales) = 0.902   <- collinear, keep one\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Redundancy check: temperature and iced_drink_sales carry almost the same signal\n",
    "print('r(temperature, iced_drink_sales) =',\n",
    "      round(cafe['temperature_c'].corr(cafe['iced_drink_sales']), 3),\n",
    "      '  <- collinear, keep one')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "858d274b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "$r \\approx 0.90$ between temperature and iced-drink sales: they carry almost the same signal (**collinearity**).\n",
    "In a simple, interpretable model we would usually keep just one of them.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Building a model\n",
    "\n",
    "We train a **random forest** (many decision trees averaged together, which can capture non-linear patterns):\n",
    "\n",
    "- `train_test_split` keeps 25 % of the days aside as a **test set** the model never sees during training.\n",
    "- The score is $R^2$ on the test set:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$R^2 = 1 - \\frac{\\sum_i (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2}{\\sum_i (y_i - \\bar{y})^2}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "  $R^2 = 1$ is a perfect prediction; $R^2 = 0$ is no better than always predicting the mean.\n",
    "- **Feature importance** shows how much each feature reduced prediction error across the trees."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "ff4abc69",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-22T08:16:04.091437Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-22T08:16:04.091143Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-22T08:16:06.139932Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-22T08:16:06.137951Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "test R² = 0.614\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
